      "id": 1,
      "text": "South Bethlehem originated when Luckenbach laid out a town-plot named Augusta in 1852, the seed of the borough.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lehighvalleyhistory.com/the-borough-of-south-bethlehem/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first South Bethlehem train station was a brick house built on Luckenbach farm in 1849, marking the start of rail service.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lehighvalleyhistory.com/the-borough-of-south-bethlehem/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The South Bethlehem Historical Society preserves the “Second Chapter” of Bethlehem’s history from 1850 onward, focusing on industrialization.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://southbethhistsoc.org/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The original station house at the junction with Lehigh Valley Road was built in 1859 and later replaced by the Union Depot in 1867.",
